  "solution_principle": "4- Weaverbird: Plugin for Mesh manipulation.\r\n\r\nGrasshopper includes basic mesh components, such as:\r\n- \"Simple Mesh\".\r\n- \"Mesh Brep\".\r\n- \"Mesh Surface\".\r\n\r\nWeaverbird allows for more options. Check out the following components:\r\n- \"Weaverbird's Loop Division\".\r\n- \"Weaverbird's Catmull-Clark Subdivision\".\r\n- \"Weaverbird's Sierpinsky Carpet\".\r\n- \"Weaverbird's Stellate/Cumulation\".\r\n- \"Weaverbird's Laplacian Smoothing\".\r\n- \"Weaverbird's Offset Mesh\".\r\n- \"Weaverbird's Mesh Thicken\".\r\n\r\n- Generate Edges of Meshes using the \"Mesh Edges\".\r\nUse new \"Curve\" components and feed in the OUTPUTS of the Mesh Edges.\r\n\r\n- Get the Face Boundaries of mesh and use them to generate new NURBS surfaces.\r\n\r\n- IMPORTANT:\r\nIf the faces are not planar, the \"Boundary Surface\" component won't work.\r\nYou may need to Triangulate all faces first.\r\n\r\n- What if we don't need planar surfaces?\r\n- In this case, extract all faces' borders (as curves), then loft opposite sides to make non-planar surfaces.",
